```{r}
library(layaR)
```

Laya answers questions it has never seen before: you describe the decision
in plain language and the model reads your wording. How you word a question
therefore matters. This vignette covers the three question types, what makes
a question work well, and the token budget a question's options share.

## The three question types

### Choice: one label from a set

Each option is a label, optionally with a description after `=`. The label
comes back as the answer, so make it meaningful; the description tells the
model what the label covers.

```{r}
dept <- laya_choice(
  "Which department should handle this request?",
  billing   = "invoices, payments, refunds",
  technical = "bugs, outages, system errors",
  sales     = "pricing, new contracts",
  other     = "everything else"
)
dept
```

Labels alone work too, when they are self-explanatory:

```{r}
laya_choice("What is the message about?", "shipping", "returns", "product question")
```

If the options live in a vector already, pass them with `.options`:

```{r}
teams <- c(billing = "invoices, refunds", technical = "bugs, outages")
laya_choice("Which team?", .options = teams)
```

### Score: a position on an ordinal scale

Give the levels from lowest to highest. The answer is the **expected
level**: a number between 0 and the number of levels minus one, so 1.6 on a
three-level scale sits between the middle and the top level.

```{r}
urgency <- laya_score("How urgent is this request?",
                      c("not urgent", "soon", "critical deadline or blocking issue"))
```

### Noul: yes or no

A statement that is either true or false about the state. The answer is the
calibrated probability that it holds. `if_false` and `if_true` can say what
each side means when that isn't obvious:

```{r}
phishing <- laya_noul("Is this email a phishing attempt?",
                      if_false = "a legitimate email",
                      if_true = "phishing or fraud")
```

## Question sets

`laya_questions()` combines questions; the argument names become the answer
ids. All the questions in a set are answered in one forward pass.

```{r}
qs <- laya_questions(department = dept, urgency = urgency,
                     churn_risk = laya_noul("Does the customer say they will cancel or leave?"))
```

Sets combine with `c()`, and `[` selects questions by id:

```{r}
more <- c(qs, laya_questions(phishing = phishing))
names(more)
names(more[c("urgency", "phishing")])
```

`laya_validate_questions()` checks a set with the engine's own rules,
without loading a model:

```{r}
shared <- '{"team": {"type": "choice", "instructions": "Which team?",
                     "criteria": ["billing", "support", "billing"]}}'
try(laya_validate_questions(shared))
```

## Writing questions that work

**Make each option self-contained.** The model sees the options, not your
database. "billing: invoices, payments, refunds" works; "team_7" does not.
Put the fact that distinguishes an option near the start of its label or
description.

**Option order matters.** The model scores one marker per option, in order,
and position alone shifts the scores: upstream measured a clear lean toward
the first options when every option reads the same. A clear-cut text gets
the same answer in any order, but an ambiguous one may not. Here the same
three options are asked in two orders:

```{r}
options_a <- c(billing = "invoices, payments, refunds", technical = "bugs, outages, login",
               sales = "pricing, new contracts")
options_b <- options_a[c("sales", "technical", "billing")]
ask <- function(text) {
  qs <- laya_questions(
    order_a = laya_choice("Which department should handle this request?", .options = options_a),
    order_b = laya_choice("Which department should handle this request?", .options = options_b)
  )
  as.data.frame(laya_predict(text, qs))[, c("id", "answer", "value")]
}
ask("We want to upgrade to 50 seats but the payment form keeps failing.")
ask("The app shows the wrong price at checkout.")
```

The second message could reasonably go to billing or to sales, and the
answer follows whichever of the two is listed first. When a wrong answer is
costly, ask in a couple of orders on your own examples, and treat answers
that change with the order as uncertain. Put a catch-all such as "other"
last. The probabilities always come back keyed by your labels, whatever the
order.

**Ask about the state, not the world.** "Does the customer say they will
cancel?" is answerable from the text; "Will the customer cancel?" asks the
model to guess. Negated wording ("Is this *not* spam?") is a known weak spot;
prefer the positive statement and read `1 - p` if you need the opposite.

**Reword a question that misses.** Small changes in wording can matter a lot.
Here three wordings of the same yes/no question are asked about texts where
the answer is known:

```{r}
texts <- c(
  "Duplicate charge. We were billed twice for March. Refund it or we cancel.",
  "Your service has been unreliable for months. Please close my account.",
  "Please refund the duplicate today or we will cancel our plan.",
  "Since the last update the app closes as soon as I log in.",
  "We're a team of 50 and want a quote for the annual plan."
)
truth <- c(TRUE, TRUE, TRUE, FALSE, FALSE)
wordings <- laya_questions(
  threaten = laya_noul("Does the user threaten to cancel or leave?"),
  say      = laya_noul("Does the customer say they will cancel or leave?"),
  mention  = laya_noul("Does the customer mention cancelling their plan, subscription or account?")
)
p <- sapply(laya_predict_batch(texts, wordings), function(r) {
  vapply(r$answers, function(a) a$noul, numeric(1))
})
data.frame(cancels = truth, round(t(p), 2))
```

"Threaten" misses every cancellation. "Say they will" gets all five right,
though the crash report sits closer to 0.5 than you might like. "Mention"
scores the cancellations highest but also flags the crash report. No wording
is perfect, and the only way to know is to measure: keep a handful of
labelled examples like these for each question you rely on, and check every
new wording against them.

**Keep a choice to about 20 options.** All the options of a question share a
fixed token budget (below). With many more, each option gets only a few
tokens and they stop being distinguishable. Split a large label set into a
coarse question and a fine one instead.

**Check calibration on your data before gating.** Confidence is calibrated
on the training distribution. Before relying on `min_confidence`, look at a
few dozen labelled examples from your own workflow and check that the
answers given with high confidence are mostly right.

## The token budget

A checkpoint reads at most `max_len` tokens: your options (the "head") and
the state share them. The options may use up to `head_max_len` tokens; the
state gets what's left. `laya_engine_info()` shows the budgets:

```{r}
info <- laya_engine_info(laya_default_engine())
info[c("checkpoint", "max_len", "head_max_len")]
```

A state too long for the room left is truncated, and the result says so:

```{r}
long_text <- strrep("The invoice was wrong again. ", 400)
res <- laya_predict(long_text, qs)
str(res$usage[c("input_tokens", "truncated")])
```

For long documents, use `laya_predict_long()`, which scans the text in
overlapping windows (see `vignette("data-frames", package = "layaR")`).

If the options don't fit at all, the call fails with a
`laya_error_no_markers` error you can catch:

```{r}
tryCatch(
  laya_predict("Hello", qs, max_len = 32),
  laya_error_no_markers = function(e) cat("Options don't fit:", conditionMessage(e), "\n")
)
```

`max_len` and `head_max_len` can be raised per call (up to 8192), which helps
when a question has many long options. The checkpoints were trained at their
shipped budgets, so measure accuracy on your own examples if you change
them.

## Sharing questions with Python

Question sets are stored in the Python SDK's format, so they move between
the two languages unchanged:

```{r}
cat(laya_questions_to_json(qs["churn_risk"]))
path <- tempfile(fileext = ".json")
laya_questions_to_json(qs, path)
identical(as_laya_questions(path), qs)
```

`as_laya_questions()` also reads a JSON string or a list in the same format.
